-\subsection{Backtest design}
-
-\paragraph{Timing and look-ahead bias.} Weights chosen at the close of day $t$ use only returns up to and including day $t$, and they earn the returns of day $t+1$: $r_{\text{port},t+1} = \sum_i w_{i,t} r_{i,t+1}$. We verified this directly: re-running the backtest on data truncated at December 2011 leaves every earlier position unchanged, up to floating-point rounding of order $10^{-13}$. Residual snapback follows the same convention and additionally fits its factor model only through day $t-1$. Between weekly rebalances its positions drift with prices and are not traded.
-
-\paragraph{Transaction costs.} The assignment defines returns without costs, so we report results both without fees and with a cost of 5 basis points per dollar traded.
-
-\paragraph{Survivorship bias.} Every stock in the sample trades continuously from 2000 to 2023, so the universe consists of firms that survived the full period. Firms that failed or were acquired are missing. This likely flatters all three strategies, since buying a stock after a fall never ends in a delisting in this sample. Residual snapback is the most exposed, because buying stocks right after unusually large drops is its entire signal. This is a limitation of the data, not of the strategy code.
-
