-\paragraph{In-sample vs.\ out-of-sample.} The difference is dramatic (Figures~\ref{fig:k-tb} and~\ref{fig:k-pairs}). In-sample, following alpha appears highly profitable, with Sharpe ratios of 2 to 3.4 that rise as more factors are added. This is look-ahead bias: the full-sample alpha is estimated from the very returns being traded, so stocks with high alpha mechanically earned high returns over the period. Out-of-sample, the same signal points the \emph{opposite} way: high past alpha predicts low future returns. An in-sample assessment would not just overstate performance; it would recommend the wrong strategy. Pairs trading is also inflated in-sample (Sharpe up to 2.4 vs.\ about 1.5 out-of-sample), though its direction is unchanged. Long-only residual snapback is the least affected (Figure~\ref{fig:k-snap}): its in-sample Sharpe ratios exceed out-of-sample ones by at most 0.18. Its signal is a short-lived residual relative to a stock's own recent volatility, which full-sample coefficients barely change, rather than an estimate of the long-run alpha that look-ahead contaminates directly. The hedged version is affected much more, increasingly so as $K$ grows (1.84 vs.\ 1.38 at $K = 5$, 1.97 vs.\ 0.46 at $K = 20$). Its hedge relies on the betas, and full-sample betas describe the factor exposures over the very period being traded, so in-sample the hedge removes factor risk almost perfectly. Out-of-sample, each extra factor adds another set of estimated betas to hedge against, and their estimation error accumulates.
