+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR We build three strategies on top of the PCA factor model. The first, a \emph{contrarian factor-neutral Treynor--Black portfolio}, trades directly on the estimated alphas $\alpha_i$. The second, \emph{PCA pairs trading}, uses the principal component loadings to find pairs of economically similar stocks and trades the convergence of their prices. The third, \emph{residual snapback}, buys stocks whose recent returns fell far short of what the factor model predicted, betting that the overreaction reverses.
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR The data are daily returns for $N = 1{,}201$ stocks from January 2000 to December 2023. All three strategies are simulated out-of-sample: every estimate is recomputed on a trailing window as the simulation moves forward. Trading begins in January 2002 once 500 days of history are available (April 2002 for residual snapback, which also needs a residual history; see Section~\ref{sec:snapback}).
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR \subsection{Rolling factor model}
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR \begin{itemize}
+% PRIOR     \item \textbf{Principal components.} Every 5 trading days, we run PCA on the correlation matrix of the previous 500 days of returns. Standardizing returns first prevents high-volatility stocks from dominating the components. We keep the top $K = 5$ eigenvectors $v_1, \dots, v_5$.
+% PRIOR     \item \textbf{Factor returns.} Each eigenvector defines an eigenportfolio with return $f^k_t = \sum_i \frac{v_k[i]}{\bar\sigma_i} r_{i,t}$.
+% PRIOR     \item \textbf{Alphas and betas.} Each day, for each stock, we regress the previous 250 days of returns on the five factor returns,
+% PRIOR     \[
+% PRIOR         r_{i,t} = \alpha_i + \sum_{k=1}^{K} \beta_{k,i} f^k_t + \epsilon_{i,t},
+% PRIOR     \]
+% PRIOR     which gives the alpha $\alpha_i$, the factor betas $\beta_{k,i}$, and the residual variance $\sigma^2(\epsilon_i)$.
+% PRIOR \end{itemize}
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR \subsection{Strategy 1: Contrarian factor-neutral Treynor--Black}
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR \paragraph{Scores.} Under the Treynor--Black model, the optimal active weight on a stock is proportional to its alpha divided by its idiosyncratic variance: a large alpha on a very noisy stock deserves less weight than a moderate alpha on a stable one. We use the negative of this ratio,
+% PRIOR \[
+% PRIOR     s_i = -\frac{\alpha_i}{\sigma^2(\epsilon_i)}.
+% PRIOR \]
+% PRIOR The sign is flipped because, out of sample, stocks with high estimated alpha go on to \emph{underperform} (Table~\ref{tab:development}). Past alpha in this universe reverses: it reflects temporary mispricing or estimation noise, not persistent outperformance. Section~\ref{sec:holdout} confirms that this reversal is already present using only 2002--2017 data. To keep a few extreme stocks from dominating, we convert the scores to cross-sectional z-scores and cap them at $\pm 3$.
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR \paragraph{Factor neutrality.} We project the scores onto the space orthogonal to a vector of ones and the $K$ beta vectors, so the portfolio satisfies
+% PRIOR \[
+% PRIOR     \sum_i w_i = 0 \quad \text{(dollar neutral)}, \qquad \sum_i w_i \beta_{k,i} = 0 \quad \text{for } k = 1, \dots, K \quad \text{(factor neutral)}.
+% PRIOR \]
+% PRIOR Because the first principal component is essentially the market, this also makes the strategy approximately market neutral.
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR \paragraph{Scaling and trading.} Weights are scaled so gross exposure is $\sum_i |w_i| = 1$ (50\% long, 50\% short), and the portfolio is rebalanced daily.
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR Each design choice helps. Reversing the sign turns a losing strategy into a profitable one. Weighting by $\alpha_i / \sigma^2(\epsilon_i)$ improves on equal weights. The factor hedge leaves the return almost unchanged (2.5\% vs.\ 2.4\%) but lowers volatility from 3.2\% to 2.9\% and market beta from 0.07 to 0.04: it removes unrewarded factor risk without sacrificing alpha. Market beta is measured against an equal-weighted average of all stocks in the universe.
