+% PRIOR \subsection{Strategy 2: PCA pairs trading}
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR \paragraph{Pair selection.} Every 21 trading days, we describe each stock by its loadings on the $K$ principal components, $\ell_i = (v_1[i]\sqrt{\lambda_1}, \dots, v_K[i]\sqrt{\lambda_K})$. We then greedily match the closest stocks in this space, keeping the 50 best non-overlapping pairs. Because the two stocks in a pair have nearly identical factor exposure, the spread between them is approximately factor neutral and driven mostly by stock-specific news.
+% PRIOR 
+% PRIOR \paragraph{Trading rule.} For each pair $(i, j)$, we track the log price ratio $\log(P_i / P_j)$ over the previous 60 days and compute its z-score $z$. When $z < -2$ we buy $i$ and short $j$; when $z > 2$ we do the reverse. We close the position once $|z| < 0.5$. Each pair is allocated a fixed $1/50$ of capital, so idle pairs hold cash.
