-\subsection{Execution and trading costs}
-
-Our simulator applies daily returns to existing positions before executing
-the next portfolio adjustment. Positions carried into day \(t\) therefore
-earn that day's returns, while newly chosen weights \(w_{i,t}\) earn
-returns on day \(t+1\):
-\[
-    r_{\mathrm{port},t+1}^{\mathrm{gross}}
-    = \sum_i w_{i,t}r_{i,t+1}.
-\]
-Strategies hold cash until their required estimates are available, after
-which we assume execution at the close used to calculate the signal.
-This approximates observing prices immediately before the close and
-submitting orders in time, but abstracts from processing delays and
-differences between observed and executed prices. Introducing an additional
-day of delay tests how strongly performance depends on immediate execution.
-
-We calculate turnover from the weights after market movements to the new
-targets, capturing the trades needed to restore an allocation even when
-its target is unchanged. Positions held without adjustment incur no
-rebalancing charge. The baseline deducts 5 basis points per dollar bought
-or sold, including both entry and exit, while comparisons at higher fee
-levels assess the sensitivity of net returns to execution costs.
-
-\subsection{Strategy development and evaluation}
-
-We divide evaluation into chronological training, validation, and final
-holdout periods, using the training sample to develop signals and compare
-candidate settings. Validation then assesses their performance on later
-observations and informs the final selection. This chronological structure
-preserves the order in which information becomes available, whereas a
-random split would allow later market conditions to influence choices
-evaluated on earlier observations.
-
-Before the final holdout, we fix the trading rules, parameter settings,
-cost assumptions, missing-data treatment, and performance measures, then
-evaluate the holdout exactly once and retain the result regardless of
-performance. This separates the final assessment from strategy selection.
-Model estimates continue to update as observations arrive, but their
-update rules remain fixed. A 500-day window, for example, continues to
-advance without its length being reconsidered in response to holdout
-results.
-
-Each strategy section follows the same sequence so readers can compare
-the evidence:
-\begin{enumerate}
-    \item \textbf{Strategy specification.} Explain the signal's motivation
-    and conversion into weights, specifying estimation windows, refitting
-    and trading intervals, and exposure limits.
-    \item \textbf{Performance.} Report gross and net cumulative returns,
-    annual results, drawdowns, and turnover to identify concentration in
-    exceptional years and the contribution of trading costs.
-    \item \textbf{Parameter sensitivity.} Vary nearby settings and factor
-    count \(K\) over common dates after the longest warm-up. Compare
-    full-sample and rolling estimates to assess how future information
-    affects performance and the apparent preferred factor count.
-    \item \textbf{Signal validation.} Compare subsequent returns across
-    signal-strength groups and holding periods, accounting for dependence
-    between overlapping returns when assessing uncertainty.
-    \item \textbf{Implementation sensitivity.} Test higher fees, delayed
-    execution, and exposure drift to identify dependence on low costs,
-    immediate fills, or unintended risk.
-    \item \textbf{Final holdout.} Report the fixed strategy's holdout result
-    alongside training and validation performance, including deterioration.
-\end{enumerate}
-
-We report Sharpe ratios using daily returns, a zero risk-free-rate
-convention, and annualization by \(\sqrt{252}\). For the assignment's
-information ratio, we subtract fitted market exposure from portfolio
-returns, divide their mean by their standard deviation, and apply the
-same annualization. We state the benchmark and report market beta so
-readers can see whether profits come with market exposure.
-
-\subsection{Limitations}
-
-Our tests address information timing, parameter sensitivity, and execution
-assumptions, but the following biases and modeling limits remain relevant
-when interpreting performance.
-
-\begin{itemize}
-    \item \textbf{Multiple-testing bias and validation overfitting.}
-    Searching across signals and settings increases the chance of selecting
-    a result that partly reflects luck. Repeated validation also makes
-    those observations part of strategy selection, even when individual
-    backtests use rolling estimates. Sensitivity checks and a final holdout
-    strengthen the evidence without making the selected historical Sharpe
-    ratio an unbiased estimate of future performance.
-
-    \item \textbf{Survivorship and universe-selection bias.} The original
-    panel contains long histories for 1,201 stocks over 2000--2023 and may
-    underrepresent failed firms, delisted securities, and later entrants.
-    Its selection procedure is unverified, so we cannot establish that
-    the universe matches the securities available on each historical date.
-    Rolling estimation restricts the information entering a fit but does
-    not correct selection of the stocks themselves.
-
-    \item \textbf{Execution and transaction-cost optimism.} Closing-price
-    execution assumes negligible processing time and access to the observed
-    price. Proportional fees omit market impact and stock-specific spreads,
-    while the baseline also omits borrow costs and availability and assumes
-    zero cash interest. These simplifications can overstate implementable
-    returns. Higher-fee and delayed-execution tests assess sensitivity but
-    do not establish achievable fills at a given portfolio size.
-
-    \item \textbf{Regime dependence and limited external validity.} The
-    final holdout covers one subsequent combination of volatility, liquidity,
-    and investor behavior. Annual comparisons reveal concentration in
-    particular episodes, but a successful holdout does not establish
-    performance under different market conditions. Conclusions therefore
-    apply to the observed periods without implying stable future returns.
-\end{itemize}
+The original universe's selection procedure is unverified and may omit failed firms and later entrants. Closing-price fills, proportional fees, and the omission of impact and stock-specific borrow costs can overstate implementable returns. Repeated testing can also overfit historical periods. These limits remain relevant even when estimates use only past data. Appendix~\ref{app:framework} gives the implementation, walk-forward diagrams, numerical benchmarks and fuller discussion.
